window.C1 = window.C1 || {};
C1.practice = C1.practice || [];
C1.practice.push({
  id: 'reading', title: 'Reading comprehension', short: 'Read a full article and answer detailed multiple-choice questions.',
  exam: 'Cambridge C1 Advanced: Reading and Use of English, Part 5 · Linguaskill: extended reading',
  instruction: 'Read the text and choose the best answer.',
  strategy: `<ol><li>Read the whole text quickly for the <strong>main line of argument</strong> before you look at the questions.</li>
    <li>Questions follow the order of the text. Underline the lines each question points to, then read them again <strong>slowly</strong>.</li>
    <li>The correct option <strong>paraphrases</strong> the text; a wrong option often repeats exact words from the text but changes the meaning (too strong, reversed, or not stated).</li>
    <li>For reference words (<em>this, that, they, which</em>) go back and try your answer in place of the word. For attitude questions, look for the writer's own evaluation, not just facts.</li></ol>`,
  sets: [
    { title: 'Set 1: The junction that stopped telling people what to do', items: [
      { type: 'text', paras: [
        `When the council of a mid-sized northern town announced plans to remove the traffic lights from its busiest junction, the reaction was predictable. Shopkeepers forecast chaos, drivers wrote angry letters to the local paper, and one councillor described the scheme as "an experiment conducted on the public". The bus company said only that it would "monitor the situation". Two years on, the junction is quieter, no less busy, and, by most accounts, safer.`,
        `The idea behind it is not new. Traffic engineers in several European countries have long argued that the more a street tells people what to do, the less they think for themselves. Signs, barriers and signals, the argument runs, lull drivers into a false sense of security; they obey the rules to the letter and stop looking at the people around them. Remove the rules, and everyone has to make eye contact, negotiate and slow down. It sounds like a recipe for disaster, yet the evidence from towns that have tried it is surprisingly encouraging.`,
        `Not everybody is convinced. Disability campaigners point out that a street without clear crossings can be intimidating, even dangerous, for people who cannot rely on eye contact with a driver. Their concerns have been taken seriously in the town in question, where textured paving and designated quiet zones have been added at the edges of the junction. Whether such compromises preserve the spirit of the original idea is debatable, but they have at least kept the critics at the table.`,
        `What has struck the designers most is how quickly behaviour changed. Within weeks, drivers who had once sped up to beat a red light were rolling through at walking pace, and pedestrians who used to wait obediently at the kerb were strolling across at whatever point suited them. Journey times, which were expected to lengthen, have barely changed, largely because the constant stop-start of the old system has disappeared. "We did not so much redesign a junction as rewrite an unspoken contract," says the project's lead architect. "People simply worked out the new terms for themselves."`,
        `That, perhaps, is the real lesson. Good urban design is rarely about dramatic gestures; it is about understanding how people already behave and then nudging them, gently, towards something better. The junction is still not beautiful. But nobody now describes it as an experiment, and the councillor who once did has quietly stopped mentioning it altogether. Other councils have sent delegations to watch, notebooks in hand, though the architect warns them against simply copying the layout.`
      ] },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the writer imply about the reaction to the scheme in paragraph 1?`,
        options: [`The scheme was abandoned soon after it began.`, `The council had consulted local residents before announcing it.`, `The predictions of disaster were not borne out by events.`, `Only the local bus company supported the plan.`],
        answer: 2, why: `The last sentence says the junction is now "quieter, no less busy, and, by most accounts, safer", so the forecasts of "chaos" proved wrong. The scheme was not abandoned (it is still there "two years on"); nothing is said about prior consultation (the reaction was to an <em>announcement</em>, and the "experiment on the public" comment suggests the opposite); and the bus company merely said it would monitor the situation, which is not support.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `According to the argument described in paragraph 2, why can traffic signals make streets more dangerous?`,
        options: [`Drivers deliberately take more risks when signals are present.`, `Drivers pay less attention to the people around them.`, `Pedestrians become too confident to cross carefully.`, `Drivers are confused by the number of signs.`],
        answer: 1, why: `The text says signals "lull drivers into a false sense of security; they obey the rules to the letter and stop looking at the people around them". The problem is inattention, not deliberate risk-taking (A), not pedestrians (C), and not confusion (D): the writer talks about obedience, not confusion.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What is the purpose of paragraph 3?`,
        options: [`To show that the scheme has been abandoned after protests.`, `To argue that campaigners are exaggerating a minor problem.`, `To suggest that the compromise has weakened the original idea.`, `To acknowledge a serious objection and explain how it was answered.`],
        answer: 3, why: `The paragraph presents the campaigners' concern ("intimidating, even dangerous") and says it "has been taken seriously", with paving and quiet zones added. The scheme has not been abandoned (A); the concern is treated as genuine, not exaggerated (B); and whether the compromise weakens the idea is merely called "debatable", so the writer does not claim this (C).` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the architect mean by "rewrite an unspoken contract" (paragraph 4)?`,
        options: [`Road users adjusted their shared expectations without being told what to do.`, `The council introduced a formal code of conduct for drivers and pedestrians.`, `Drivers and pedestrians signed an agreement organised by the designers.`, `The new layout made the rules of the junction easier to read.`],
        answer: 0, why: `"Unspoken" and "People simply worked out the new terms for themselves" show that nobody issued rules; behaviour changed informally. B, C and D all involve explicit, written or visible rules, which is exactly what the scheme removed.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What is the writer's attitude to the project in paragraph 5?`,
        options: [`Unreservedly enthusiastic about its appearance and success.`, `Broadly positive but aware of its limits.`, `Doubtful that other towns will ever copy it.`, `Critical of the councillor for changing his mind.`],
        answer: 1, why: `"The junction is still not beautiful" is a limit, but the objectors have gone quiet and "other councils have sent delegations", so the tone is positive. A ignores "still not beautiful"; C is contradicted by the delegations; D over-reads the mild remark that the councillor "quietly stopped mentioning it".` }
    ] },

    { title: 'Set 2: Why we remember what never happened', items: [
      { type: 'text', paras: [
        `Ask people where they were when they first heard some momentous piece of news, and most will answer instantly and with great confidence. They will describe the room, the weather, even what they were wearing. Psychologists call these "flashbulb memories", and their vividness is the very reason they are so trusted. The trouble is that, when researchers compare people's accounts over time, the details drift. Confidence, however, does not.`,
        `This mismatch lies at the heart of a broader shift in how memory is understood. The old picture, in which the brain stores experiences like files in a cabinet, to be retrieved intact whenever needed, has been largely abandoned. In its place is a more unsettling view: each time we recall something, we reconstruct it, drawing on fragments of the original event, general knowledge and whatever we happen to believe at the time. The memory is then stored again, subtly altered. Neuroscientists have found that remembering activates much of the same circuitry as imagining, which may help explain why the two so easily blur.`,
        `One consequence is that memories can be contaminated. In a series of well-known experiments, volunteers were shown footage of a minor car accident and later asked how fast the vehicles were going when they "smashed" into each other, or merely "hit" each other. Those given the more dramatic verb estimated higher speeds and were more likely to recall broken glass that had never been there. The phrasing of a question, it seems, can leave a residue in the memory it is supposed to be probing.`,
        `None of this means that memory is worthless. For most everyday purposes, reconstruction works remarkably well; it is fast, flexible and good at capturing the gist of an event, which is usually what we need. Nor is inaccuracy a sign of a faulty brain: a system that recorded the past perfectly would probably struggle to make sense of the present. Problems arise mainly when we treat memory as evidence, as in a courtroom, where a witness's certainty can persuade a jury far more than it should. Several legal systems have consequently revised their guidance on how witnesses are questioned.`,
        `For the rest of us, the lesson may be one of modesty. It would be foolish to distrust every recollection, but when we find ourselves insisting that we remember something exactly, that very insistence is worth examining. Feeling certain, after all, is a sensation, not a measurement. Families who argue over who remembers a holiday correctly might take some comfort: they are probably both partly wrong, and both quite sure.`
      ] },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What point does the writer make about flashbulb memories in paragraph 1?`,
        options: [`They fade more slowly than ordinary memories do.`, `People stay sure of them even though the details change.`, `They are less vivid than people usually claim.`, `Research has shown them to be entirely invented.`],
        answer: 1, why: `"The details drift. Confidence, however, does not." means belief stays firm while accuracy declines. Nothing is said about the speed of fading (A); vividness is described as real ("their vividness is the very reason they are so trusted"), so C is wrong; "entirely invented" (D) is far too strong: only the <em>details</em> drift.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does "This mismatch" in paragraph 2 refer to?`,
        options: [`the difference between old and new theories of memory`, `the gap between researchers and the general public`, `the contrast between how certain people feel about a memory and how accurate it is`, `the disagreement between psychologists about flashbulb memories`],
        answer: 2, why: `The mismatch is the one just described: details drift but confidence does not. The old-versus-new theories (A) are what the mismatch "lies at the heart of", so they cannot be the mismatch itself. B and D are not mentioned anywhere.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What point does the writer illustrate with the car-accident experiments in paragraph 3?`,
        options: [`The wording of a question can change what people remember.`, `People consistently overestimate the speed of vehicles.`, `Witnesses to accidents tend to remember broken glass.`, `Dramatic events are remembered more accurately than minor ones.`],
        answer: 0, why: `"The phrasing of a question, it seems, can leave a residue in the memory" states the point. The speed estimates differed by verb, not by a general tendency to overestimate (B); the broken glass "had never been there", so it was a false memory, not an accurate one (C); and the accident was "minor", so D is unsupported.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What is the writer's view of reconstructive memory in paragraph 4?`,
        options: [`It is a serious flaw that should be corrected wherever possible.`, `It is reliable in courtrooms but not in daily life.`, `It should be replaced by written records in most situations.`, `It is generally useful, and causes trouble mainly when treated as proof.`],
        answer: 3, why: `"Works remarkably well", "fast, flexible" and "Problems arise mainly when we treat memory as evidence" together give this balanced view. A contradicts "not a sign of a faulty brain"; B reverses the courtroom point; C is never suggested.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the writer mean by saying that feeling certain is "a sensation, not a measurement"?`,
        options: [`We should not trust anything we remember.`, `Strong feelings usually indicate an accurate memory.`, `How sure we feel tells us nothing reliable about how accurate we are.`, `Scientists can measure certainty more precisely than they can measure memory.`],
        answer: 2, why: `A "sensation" is how something feels; a "measurement" would be a reliable check. The paragraph opens by saying "It would be foolish to distrust every recollection", which rules out A; B is the opposite of the argument; D is not discussed.` }
    ] },

    { title: 'Set 3: In defence of "literally"', items: [
      { type: 'text', paras: [
        `Every few years, a new outrage sweeps through the letters pages and social media feeds of English-speaking countries: somebody has used a word wrongly. The most persistent offender is "literally", now routinely used to add emphasis to statements that are plainly figurative, as in "I was literally dying of embarrassment". Purists are appalled. I am not, and I want to explain why. The debate usually generates far more heat than light, and it rarely ends with anyone changing their mind.`,
        `Consider how many of the words we use today have travelled a long way from their origins. "Nice" once meant foolish; "awful" meant inspiring awe; "silly" meant blessed. Nobody now regards these shifts as errors, still less as evidence of decline, yet each one must have irritated somebody at the time. The same process is at work in words as ordinary as "decimate", "awesome" and "terrific". The complaint that a word is being misused is, in effect, a complaint that language has failed to stand still, and it never does.`,
        `Critics of "literally" have a better case than most, I admit. A word that can mean both "in the exact sense" and "not in the exact sense" seems to have lost its value, because listeners can no longer be sure which is intended. But this overlooks how language copes. Ambiguity of this kind is hardly rare: "cleave" means both "to split" and "to stick together", and the language has survived it. Speakers rely on context, tone and shared knowledge to work out what is meant, and they rarely misunderstand one another. Hardly anyone hearing that a colleague was literally dying of embarrassment has called an ambulance.`,
        `Moreover, the emphatic use of "literally" is not a modern invention. Writers of the nineteenth century, including some who are now held up as models of good prose, used it in exactly this way. The supposed corruption is, therefore, older than most of the people complaining about it. Its recent prominence owes less to a sudden change in behaviour than to the fact that everyone's speech is now more widely recorded, shared and, inevitably, criticised.`,
        `None of this is an argument for carelessness. Precision matters in a contract, a manual or a medical leaflet, and those who teach writing are right to insist on it. But a language is not a legal document. It belongs to those who use it, and the only sensible test of a word is whether it does its job. If enough people understand "literally" as a booster, then a booster is what it is, and mourning the old meaning will change nothing. That may be uncomfortable for those of us who like our grammar tidy, but it is also what keeps a language alive.`
      ] },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What is the writer's position at the end of paragraph 1?`,
        options: [`He shares the purists' anger but thinks it is exaggerated.`, `He does not regard the use of "literally" as a problem.`, `He thinks the usage is incorrect but harmless.`, `He has not yet decided whether the usage matters.`],
        answer: 1, why: `"Purists are appalled. I am not" is a clear statement that he is not troubled by it. He does not share the anger (A), never calls the usage incorrect (C: the rest of the piece argues it is legitimate), and he is not undecided (D): he "wants to explain why".` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `Why does the writer mention "nice", "awful" and "silly" in paragraph 2?`,
        options: [`To show that words tend to lose their meaning over time.`, `To prove that modern speakers are more careless than earlier ones.`, `To suggest that changes now seen as normal once provoked similar complaints.`, `To demonstrate that purists do not understand word origins.`],
        answer: 2, why: `"Each one must have irritated somebody at the time" is the point. He never says words become meaningless (A), nor that today's speakers are worse (B: the opposite of his view), and he does not accuse purists of ignorance of etymology (D), which would be a much stronger claim than the text makes.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does "this" refer to in "But this overlooks how language copes" (paragraph 3)?`,
        options: [`the claim that "literally" has lost its usefulness because its meaning is unclear`, `the writer's own admission that critics have a good case`, `the way speakers use context to work out what is meant`, `the existence of words with two opposite meanings`],
        answer: 0, why: `The sentence before gives the critics' reasoning: the word "seems to have lost its value, because listeners can no longer be sure which is intended". It is this argument that overlooks how language copes. B is what the writer concedes, not what "overlooks" anything; C is what is overlooked, not the thing overlooking it; D is a fact, not an argument that can overlook something.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `According to paragraph 4, why does the emphatic use of "literally" seem a recent problem?`,
        options: [`The word was invented only recently.`, `Nineteenth-century writers avoided using it.`, `More people now write carelessly than in the past.`, `Everyday speech is now recorded and shared far more widely.`],
        answer: 3, why: `The paragraph ends: "everyone's speech is now more widely recorded, shared and, inevitably, criticised." A and B are contradicted by the nineteenth-century examples; C is not stated.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the writer conclude in the final paragraph?`,
        options: [`Precision should be given up in all forms of writing.`, `Whether a word works for its users should decide whether it is acceptable, although precision matters in some texts.`, `Teachers of writing are wrong to insist on precision.`, `The traditional meaning of "literally" is likely to return.`],
        answer: 1, why: `"The only sensible test of a word is whether it does its job", yet "precision matters in a contract, a manual or a medical leaflet". A and C contradict "None of this is an argument for carelessness" and "those who teach writing are right"; D contradicts "mourning the old meaning will change nothing".` }
    ] },

    { title: 'Set 4: The mind at match point', items: [
      { type: 'text', paras: [
        `Watch a tennis player prepare to serve in the deciding game of a tournament and you can almost see the internal argument. The shoulders tense, the ball bounces one time too many, the gaze flickers towards the crowd. Commentators, of course, call it nerves. Physically, nothing has changed since the first game; the player is no less fit, no less skilled. What has changed is what the moment means, and that, according to sport psychologists, is where most contests are decided.`,
        `For decades, coaches treated the mental side of sport as a matter of temperament: some athletes had "nerve" and others did not. That view has been steadily replaced by the idea that composure is a skill, and like any skill it can be trained. Meditation, breathing exercises and visualisation all feature in the modern athlete's toolkit, though not all have been rigorously tested. Techniques vary, but most share a common aim, which is to shift attention away from outcomes, which the athlete cannot control, and towards processes, which they can. A golfer who is thinking about the trophy is not thinking about the putt.`,
        `A curious finding concerns what happens when experts start to think too hard about what they are doing. Skilled performers, it turns out, tend to do best when their movements run on something close to autopilot, and worst when they consciously monitor each step. A pianist who suddenly becomes aware of her own fingers will often stumble over passages she has played thousands of times. Researchers have coined the term "choking" for this collapse, and it occurs most often when the stakes are high and the athlete is being watched. Ironically, the desire to do well is what interferes with doing well.`,
        `Several remedies are now widely used. Some athletes rehearse a brief routine before each attempt, such as bouncing a ball a set number of times, which gives the mind something concrete to focus on. Others use a "cue word" chosen to trigger a calm, fluent movement. Still others practise deliberately under pressure, inviting spectators to watch or attaching small penalties to failure, so that the real occasion feels less unfamiliar. None of these methods is magic; they work, when they do, by making the unusual feel routine. Which of them suits a given individual seems to depend largely on personality, and trial and error remains the usual method of finding out.`,
        `There is, however, a note of caution. Critics argue that the popularity of mental training risks turning every defeat into a psychological failing, when sometimes the opponent is simply better. Most practitioners accept the point. A calm mind, they say, cannot make up for a lack of ability; it merely ensures that ability is available when it is needed most.`
      ] },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does "that" refer to in "and that, according to sport psychologists, is where most contests are decided" (paragraph 1)?`,
        options: [`the player's level of fitness`, `the significance the player attaches to the situation`, `the behaviour of the crowd`, `the technique the player uses to serve`],
        answer: 1, why: `The sentence contrasts what has <em>not</em> changed (fitness, skill) with "what the moment means", and "that" points back to this meaning. Fitness (A) and skill/technique (D) are explicitly said to be unchanged; the crowd (C) is only a place the player glances.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `Why does the writer mention the golfer in paragraph 2?`,
        options: [`To show that golfers experience more pressure than other athletes.`, `To argue that winning a trophy matters less than playing well.`, `To suggest that golf demands more mental skill than other sports.`, `To illustrate that concentrating on results can distract from the task in hand.`],
        answer: 3, why: `"Not thinking about the putt" illustrates the aim of shifting attention "away from outcomes... towards processes". The text makes no comparison with other sports (A, C) and does not rank trophies against performance (B).` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the writer find ironic about "choking"?`,
        options: [`The wish to perform well can itself damage performance.`, `It happens more often when nobody is watching.`, `Highly skilled performers are worse at it than beginners.`, `Conscious thought improves movements that were once automatic.`],
        answer: 0, why: `"Ironically, the desire to do well is what interferes with doing well." B contradicts "being watched"; C misreads the text (experts choke, but beginners are not compared); D is the reverse of the finding, which is that conscious monitoring makes performance <em>worse</em>.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `According to paragraph 4, what is the purpose of practising under pressure?`,
        options: [`To eliminate nervousness completely.`, `To identify which athletes are likely to fail.`, `To make the real event feel more familiar.`, `To replace the need for a pre-performance routine.`],
        answer: 2, why: `"So that the real occasion feels less unfamiliar", and methods work "by making the unusual feel routine". "None of these methods is magic" rules out A; B is not mentioned; D is wrong because routines and pressure practice are listed side by side as separate remedies, not as replacements for each other.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `How does the writer respond to the criticism reported in paragraph 5?`,
        options: [`By dismissing it, because mental training is essential to success.`, `By rejecting it, on the grounds that talent matters less than composure.`, `By noting it, although practitioners have ignored it.`, `By accepting that it has some force, as practitioners themselves do.`],
        answer: 3, why: `"Most practitioners accept the point" and the paragraph opens with "a note of caution", so the criticism is treated as partly valid. A and B dismiss it, contradicting this; C is contradicted by "accept".` }
    ] },

    { title: 'Set 5: The last ferry', items: [
      { type: 'text', paras: [
        `The ferry comes once a fortnight, weather permitting, and the phrase "weather permitting" is not a formality. I had been on the island for nine days when I understood that the timetable was a kind of suggestion, and that nobody minded. The nearest shop of any size is a day's journey away, and the nearest hospital further still. I had come, like most visitors, hoping to escape; what I had not anticipated was how quickly the idea of escape would lose its meaning.`,
        `There are fewer than a hundred permanent residents, and they have organised their lives around a principle that visitors from cities find hard to grasp: nothing can be wasted, because nothing can be easily replaced. Fishing boats are repaired rather than retired. Timber that washes ashore is saved for barns. The single shop stocks what the islanders need rather than what they might want, and a delayed delivery means a week without flour, which everyone accepts without complaint. Even conversation seems subject to a kind of economy: people say what needs saying and then, without awkwardness, stop.`,
        `I had expected this to feel like deprivation. It didn't. Instead, I found that scarcity had produced a curious kind of generosity. Neighbours lent tools without being asked and returned them without being reminded; a woman I barely knew pressed a jar of homemade preserve into my hands on the day I left, waving away my attempts to pay. It was not sentimentality. In a community where everyone depends on everyone else, giving is simply a form of insurance, and everybody knows it.`,
        `This is not to say that the place is idyllic. The winters are long, the young leave for work and study on the mainland, and the school, which once had thirty pupils, now has four. Several islanders told me, with a directness I came to admire, that they feared for its future. The islanders themselves are divided over what to do. Some welcome the newcomers who arrive each summer with cameras and good intentions; others quietly resent them. Tourism brings money, but it also brings the risk of turning a working community into a stage set for people like me.`,
        `On my last morning I stood on the jetty with my bag, watching the ferry grow from a speck into a solid shape. A man mending nets nearby looked up and asked whether I would be back. I said I hoped so. He nodded, unconvinced, and returned to his work. I did not blame him. Visitors say such things easily, and the island has heard them all before.`
      ] },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the writer mean by saying that "the idea of escape would lose its meaning" (paragraph 1)?`,
        options: [`He found that he could not leave the island when he wished.`, `He was disappointed that the island was not remote enough.`, `The island's pace of life made the wish to get away seem beside the point.`, `He decided to stay on the island permanently.`],
        answer: 2, why: `He says the timetable was "a kind of suggestion, and nobody minded", and that he had not expected the idea of escape to fade. A suggests he was trapped, which the tone does not support; B is unrelated; D is contradicted by the last paragraph, when he leaves on the ferry.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the writer suggest about the islanders' attitude to possessions in paragraph 2?`,
        options: [`They value things highly because replacing them is difficult.`, `They dislike buying new goods on principle.`, `They are unhappy with the limited stock in the shop.`, `They share everything equally among themselves.`],
        answer: 0, why: `"Nothing can be wasted, because nothing can be easily replaced" gives the reason. It is a practical necessity, not a principle against buying (B); the delayed flour is "accepted without complaint", so C is wrong; equal sharing (D) is not mentioned in this paragraph.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does "It" refer to in "It was not sentimentality" (paragraph 3)?`,
        options: [`the writer's expectation of deprivation`, `the scarcity of goods on the island`, `the writer's attempt to pay for the preserve`, `the generosity the islanders showed`],
        answer: 3, why: `The generosity (lending tools, the gift of preserve) has just been described, and the next sentence explains its practical motive: "giving is simply a form of insurance". The expectation (A), the scarcity (B) and the attempt to pay (C) are not what could be mistaken for sentimentality.` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What is the purpose of paragraph 4?`,
        options: [`To suggest that the writer regrets his visit.`, `To balance the positive picture by describing real difficulties.`, `To criticise the islanders for resisting change.`, `To argue that tourism should be discouraged.`],
        answer: 1, why: `It opens with "This is not to say that the place is idyllic" and lists long winters, emigration and a shrinking school. The writer does not express regret (A), does not criticise the islanders, whom he admires (C), and mentions only a "risk" from tourism rather than arguing for restrictions (D).` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `Why does the man nod "unconvinced" in the last paragraph?`,
        options: [`He does not want the writer to return.`, `He believes the ferry will be delayed again.`, `He assumes the writer's promise is like those visitors usually make.`, `He thinks the writer has misunderstood island life.`],
        answer: 2, why: `The writer explains: "Visitors say such things easily, and the island has heard them all before." Nothing suggests hostility (A), the ferry is already approaching (B), or that the man judges the writer's understanding of the island (D).` }
    ] },

    { title: 'Set 6: The bin is not the first stop', items: [
      { type: 'text', paras: [
        `We have grown used to thinking of food waste as a kitchen problem: the forgotten lettuce, the half-eaten casserole, the loaf that goes stale before the weekend. Guilt, we are told, is the appropriate response, and there are plenty of websites eager to supply recipes for leftovers. This framing is comforting, because it places the solution firmly in our own hands. It is also, at best, incomplete. Yet if you follow a piece of food back along the chain from the plate, the picture changes considerably.`,
        `A great deal of food never reaches a kitchen at all. Crops are left unharvested when prices fall below the cost of picking them; vegetables that are the wrong shape or size are rejected before they leave the farm; whole batches are discarded when a delivery misses its slot. A farmer who cannot find a buyer for a field of cabbages has few alternatives to ploughing them back into the soil. Much of this waste is invisible to consumers, and is the result of decisions taken by growers, wholesalers and supermarkets who are responding, not unreasonably, to the incentives they face.`,
        `Retailers, in particular, have come in for criticism, and some of it is deserved. Strict cosmetic standards and large, bulk-buy offers encourage both suppliers and shoppers to overproduce and overbuy. Yet it would be unfair to cast them as simple villains. Customers who say they want imperfect fruit will often, faced with a shelf, pick the flawless one. It is a familiar gap between conscience and behaviour. Supermarkets, no less than the rest of us, tend to respond to what people do rather than to what they say.`,
        `This suggests that solutions will have to work at several levels. Some are already visible: schemes that redirect surplus produce to charities, apps that sell unsold food cheaply at closing time, and rules that make it easier for farmers to sell "wonky" vegetables. None is a complete answer, and each has its own difficulties, from the cost of transport to the reluctance of firms to admit how much they discard. But together they point in a helpful direction. It is also worth remembering that measures which save money tend to spread faster than those that merely inspire guilt.`,
        `What we should resist is the temptation to make individual households carry the entire moral burden. Nobody is excused from throwing less away; small habits do matter, and they add up. But a citizen who plans meals meticulously cannot, on her own, change how a harvest is priced or how an order is placed. The comfortable message that everything depends on us is, in the end, an evasion. Treating waste as a personal failing may make us feel responsible, but responsibility that is misdirected achieves very little.`
      ] },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What is the writer's view of the idea that food waste is mainly a household problem?`,
        options: [`It is appealing but leaves out much of the picture.`, `It is false and was invented by retailers.`, `It has made people feel less guilty than they should.`, `It is accurate but unhelpful.`],
        answer: 0, why: `"Comforting" explains the appeal, and "at best, incomplete" gives the criticism. He does not call it false (B), does not say it leaves people too little guilt (C: the writer says the framing makes people feel responsible, and that guilt is not the answer), and does not concede it is accurate (D).` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does the writer suggest about growers, wholesalers and supermarkets in paragraph 2?`,
        options: [`They waste food deliberately to keep prices high.`, `They are unaware how much food is thrown away.`, `They mostly ignore the incentives created by the market.`, `They are reacting sensibly to the pressures placed on them.`],
        answer: 3, why: `They are "responding, not unreasonably, to the incentives they face". So they are not deliberately wasteful (A), the text does not say they are unaware (B), and the incentives are the very thing driving their behaviour (C).` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What point does the writer make about shoppers in paragraph 3?`,
        options: [`They are unfairly blamed for the behaviour of supermarkets.`, `What they say they want is not always what they choose.`, `They generally prefer misshapen fruit to perfect fruit.`, `They buy in bulk mainly because it saves money.`],
        answer: 1, why: `"Customers who say they want imperfect fruit will often... pick the flawless one." A reverses the direction of blame; C is the opposite of what happens at the shelf; D is not stated (bulk offers are said to encourage overbuying, which is different).` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What does "they" refer to in "But together they point in a helpful direction" (paragraph 4)?`,
        options: [`the difficulties each scheme faces`, `farmers and charities`, `the schemes, apps and rules just listed`, `the different levels at which action is needed`],
        answer: 2, why: `"Together" refers to the separate measures already named (charity schemes, apps, rules), none of which is complete alone. The difficulties (A) are the thing that stops each being a full answer; farmers and charities (B) are only beneficiaries within the list; the "levels" (D) come earlier and are not what "points in a direction".` },
      { type: 'mcq', q: `What is the writer's main point in the final paragraph?`,
        options: [`Households need not worry about throwing food away.`, `Personal effort is worthwhile but should not be the only focus of responsibility.`, `Careful meal planning has no real effect on waste.`, `Guilt is the most effective way of changing behaviour.`],
        answer: 1, why: `"Nobody is excused... small habits do matter" but "responsibility that is misdirected achieves very little". A contradicts the first; C contradicts "they add up"; D is contradicted by "may make us feel responsible" being presented as insufficient.` }
    ] }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'gapped', title: 'Gapped text', short: 'Choose the removed sentence that fits each gap.',
  exam: 'Cambridge C1 Advanced: Reading and Use of English, Part 7 · Linguaskill: gapped sentences',
  instruction: 'Choose the sentence that best fills each gap.',
  strategy: `<ol><li>Read the whole passage once, ignoring the gaps, to get the overall line of argument.</li>
    <li>For each gap look <strong>before and after</strong>. Check pronouns (<em>this, they, that approach</em>), linkers (<em>however, therefore, too, yet</em>) and time expressions (<em>nowadays, at first, before long</em>): the missing sentence must connect to both sides.</li>
    <li>Reject options that <strong>contradict</strong> a neighbouring sentence, repeat information already given, or introduce a topic that the next sentence does not pick up.</li>
    <li>Read the completed passage again to check that it flows.</li></ol>`,
  sets: [
    { title: 'Set 1: The rise of the audiobook', items: [{
      type: 'passage', mode: 'mcq', title: 'The rise of the audiobook',
      text: 'For years, audiobooks were regarded as a poor relation of the printed page, something for long car journeys or for people who could not read comfortably. {1} Sales have grown steadily, and publishers now commission recordings at the same time as they print new titles. The change owes a good deal to the smartphone. {2} Listening has therefore become something that can be fitted around the rest of life, rather than something that demands its own time. Some critics remain unconvinced. They argue that listening is a passive activity and that a listener cannot pause to reread a difficult sentence in the same way. {3} Researchers who have compared the two forms have found little difference in how well people understand a story, whichever way they take it in. What does change is the experience. A skilled narrator can bring a text to life, but a poor one can ruin it. {4} Others, though, find that a favourite actor\'s reading gives them a new appreciation of a familiar book. Whatever one\'s view, the format has clearly found a place in daily habits. {5} Nobody seems to be worried, however, that the printed book is about to disappear.',
      gaps: [
        { options: ['This is why they will always remain a minority interest.', 'Their popularity had already begun to decline by then.', 'That reputation has changed dramatically in the past decade.', 'Such prejudice is still common among publishers today.'], answer: 2, why: 'The next sentence says sales have grown and publishers now commission recordings, so the gap must mark a change in reputation. A and B contradict the growth (and "This" in A has no clear referent); D contradicts "publishers now commission recordings".' },
        { options: ['Portable cassette players were a similar breakthrough in the 1980s.', 'Recordings can now be downloaded in seconds and played while commuting, cooking or exercising.', 'Many people still prefer to buy audiobooks on disc.', 'Screens, however, make it harder to concentrate on a story.'], answer: 1, why: 'The following "therefore" needs a cause linked to the smartphone: instant, portable listening explains why audiobooks fit around other activities. A shifts to the wrong period and gives no cause; C is about discs, not phones; D\'s "however" and its negative claim contradict the result that follows.' },
        { options: ['The evidence, however, does not support this concern.', 'The evidence for this view is therefore strong.', 'Readers, in contrast, are much more likely to lose concentration.', 'Some of them have even given up reading altogether.'], answer: 0, why: '"this concern" refers back to the critics\' worries, and the next sentence gives research showing "little difference". B says the opposite of that research; C makes a new claim that the research then contradicts; in D "them" has no clear referent and it does not lead to the research.' },
        { options: ['Every narrator interprets a text in much the same way.', 'Most listeners therefore choose books read by famous actors.', 'Printed books, by comparison, are often badly designed.', 'Some listeners admit to abandoning a book because they could not bear the narrator\'s voice.'], answer: 3, why: '"Others, though" needs a preceding group with a negative view of narrators; D supplies it and follows "a poor one can ruin it". A contradicts "skilled... poor"; B\'s "therefore" is unsupported and would make "Others, though" pointless; C changes topic to book design.' },
        { options: ['It is therefore certain that print will soon disappear.', 'Nevertheless, its future is now in serious doubt.', 'Industry watchers expect the appetite for listening to keep growing for some time yet.', 'Audiobooks were first recorded for blind readers in the twentieth century.'], answer: 2, why: 'The final sentence, "Nobody seems to be worried, however", contrasts continued growth with the survival of print. A and B contradict that final sentence; D is a historical fact that does not follow "Whatever one\'s view... daily habits" and does not set up "however".' }
      ]
    }] },

    { title: 'Set 2: The return of the night sky', items: [{
      type: 'passage', mode: 'mcq', title: 'The return of the night sky',
      text: 'In most cities, the stars have all but disappeared. Streetlights, illuminated signs and lit office windows combine to create a permanent glow, known as light pollution, that hides all but the brightest objects. {1} It was only when a group of astronomers began measuring the problem systematically that its extent became clear. Their findings have prompted some towns to act. {2} Lamps are now shielded so that they shine downwards, and many are dimmed or switched off in the small hours. Residents were at first uneasy about the changes. {3} Yet reported crime has not risen, and the savings on electricity have been considerable. Wildlife has also benefited. Migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate, are less likely to be drawn off course. {4} Insects, too, suffer less disorientation around the bright lamps that once attracted them in vast numbers. Not everyone is convinced, of course. Some people feel that a darker street is a less friendly one. {5} For those who have seen the Milky Way from a village that once glowed orange, the sacrifice seems a small price to pay.',
      gaps: [
        { options: ['For a long time, however, few people regarded this as a problem at all.', 'Most people therefore knew exactly how serious it was.', 'Astronomers had been measuring it for decades before that.', 'Some towns had already solved the problem entirely.'], answer: 0, why: '"It was only when... its extent became clear" implies people had not noticed the problem. B and C contradict "only when"; D contradicts the next-but-one sentence, where findings "prompted some towns to act".' },
        { options: ['That approach has not been tried anywhere in the country.', 'The astronomers, however, have since moved abroad.', 'Others have simply put more lamps along the busiest roads.', 'In one, the old streetlights have been replaced by a design that wastes far less light.'], answer: 3, why: 'The next sentence, "Lamps are now shielded", describes what such a redesign involves, so the gap must introduce the new lighting. A has no referent for "That approach" and denies that towns acted; B\'s "however" leads nowhere; C describes the opposite of shielding and dimming.' },
        { options: ['Most of them welcomed the extra darkness from the very start.', 'Many feared that darker streets would make them more vulnerable to crime.', 'Crime rates in the area had been falling for several years.', 'The changes were, however, introduced without any warning.'], answer: 1, why: '"Yet reported crime has not risen" contrasts with a worry about crime, which B supplies and which explains "uneasy". A contradicts "at first uneasy"; in C nothing is contrasted by "Yet"; D\'s "however" has no earlier point to contrast with and never mentions crime.' },
        { options: ['The birds, however, have shown no sign of changing their routes.', 'Insects were, after all, the first creatures to be affected.', 'Nocturnal mammals such as bats are also able to hunt in more natural conditions.', 'Farmers have reported no change in the number of insects.'], answer: 2, why: '"Insects, too" needs a previous, different animal group as another beneficiary; C provides one. A contradicts "less likely to be drawn off course"; B and D mention insects already, so "too" would add nothing.' },
        { options: ['That view is shared by all of the astronomers involved.', 'Others admit that safety has not suffered but still miss the cheerful glow of the old lights.', 'Such people are usually those who have never left the town.', 'The lamps, they say, should be made brighter still.'], answer: 1, why: '"The sacrifice" in the next sentence refers to something given up, here the cheerful glow of the lights, and "Others" pairs with "Some people". A and C add unsupported claims and do not name a sacrifice; D asks for a reversal of the changes and gives "the sacrifice" no referent.' }
      ]
    }] },

    { title: 'Set 3: A potter\'s second act', items: [{
      type: 'passage', mode: 'mcq', title: 'A potter\'s second act',
      text: 'Marta Weston did not intend to become a potter. For twenty years she worked as an accountant, and she was, by her own account, very good at it. {1} One weekend, a colleague invited her to a pottery class as a birthday treat, and she went along mainly to be polite. {2} By the end of the afternoon she had produced a lopsided bowl and a strong wish to try again. Within a year she was spending every evening at the wheel. Her first customers were friends, who bought her mugs out of loyalty. {3} A local café began to order dozens at a time, and word spread. When the recession forced her employer to make redundancies, she took the payment and rented a small workshop. {4} Her income fell by more than half, which she describes as the best bargain of her life. She still keeps her accounting skills, which she says have proved more useful than anyone would guess. {5} Nowadays she teaches classes of her own, and she always tells beginners that the first bowl is meant to be terrible.',
      gaps: [
        { options: ['That is why she has never regretted leaving her career.', 'She was therefore delighted when her employer offered her a promotion.', 'Her accountancy firm still employs her today.', 'Yet she had also, for some time, felt that something was missing.'], answer: 3, why: '"Yet" contrasts her success with dissatisfaction and prepares for the sudden change of "One weekend". A is out of time sequence (she has not yet left) and "That" has no referent; B\'s "therefore" is illogical; C contradicts her later career change.' },
        { options: ['She had never touched clay before and expected to be hopeless.', 'She had attended similar classes every week for years.', 'That was the last time she ever went to one.', 'The class was, however, cancelled at the last moment.'], answer: 0, why: 'Expecting to be hopeless contrasts with the "strong wish to try again". B contradicts "did not intend to become a potter" and the surprise of the next sentence; C contradicts the wish to try again; D contradicts "by the end of the afternoon she had produced a bowl".' },
        { options: ['Few of them ever bought a second mug.', 'Her mugs have since been sold in shops all over the country.', 'Before long, however, strangers were buying them simply because they liked them.', 'Loyalty, she says, is the only reason anyone buys handmade crafts.'], answer: 2, why: 'The gap needs to move from friends to a wider market, leading to the café order. A contradicts the growth in the next sentence; B jumps too far ahead in time and pre-empts the café; D contradicts the growth beyond loyal friends.' },
        { options: ['Her income rose sharply as a result.', 'Her friends were astonished, and some thought she was mad.', 'The workshop, she soon discovered, was much too large for her needs.', 'She had done so, however, before the recession began.'], answer: 1, why: 'A reaction to her risky decision fits before the statement that her income fell and she calls it a bargain. A contradicts "income fell"; C contradicts "small workshop"; D contradicts "When the recession forced..." which shows she did it afterwards.' },
        { options: ['Yet her real satisfaction comes from passing on what she has learnt.', 'She has never taught anyone else to make pottery.', 'Her former colleagues, however, have long since stopped visiting.', 'Two years ago she gave up the workshop and returned to accounting.'], answer: 0, why: '"Yet" contrasts her accounting skills with her real pleasure, and "Nowadays she teaches classes" gives an example. B contradicts the next sentence; C introduces colleagues who are never picked up; D contradicts "Nowadays she teaches" and "She still keeps".' }
      ]
    }] },

    { title: 'Set 4: The four-day week', items: [{
      type: 'passage', mode: 'mcq', title: 'The four-day week',
      text: 'The idea of a four-day working week has moved from the fringes of economics to the front pages. {1} In these trials, employees kept their full salaries while working fewer hours, and employers watched closely for any fall in output. The early results have surprised sceptics. {2} Many managers reported that staff were more focused, having less time to waste in meetings or on idle browsing. Absence through illness also fell. {3} Some businesses, for example, found that the pressure of compressing tasks into fewer days left staff exhausted rather than refreshed. {4} A hospital cannot close on Fridays, and a shop assistant cannot serve customers who are not there. Nor can a fire crew or a bus service simply be told to work fewer hours. Even so, supporters argue that the principle is worth testing more widely. {5} If the experiments continue to succeed, that assumption may soon look very old-fashioned. Habits of that kind rarely change until someone is bold enough to test them.',
      gaps: [
        { options: ['Nobody has yet tried it on any large scale.', 'Several companies have recently taken part in large trials of the idea.', 'Economists have always agreed that it is impractical.', 'The five-day week itself was introduced over a century ago.'], answer: 1, why: '"In these trials" needs a preceding mention of trials. A contradicts the existence of trials; C and D contain no trials for "these" to refer to.' },
        { options: ['Productivity has fallen so sharply that many firms have abandoned the trial.', 'Sceptics have nevertheless refused to change their minds.', 'Salaries, however, have remained lower than before.', 'Productivity, in most cases, has held steady, and in some it has even improved.'], answer: 3, why: 'The results "surprised sceptics", and the next sentence gives a reason (more focused staff). A contradicts both; B contradicts "surprised" and is not supported by the next sentence; C contradicts the previous sentence, where employees "kept their full salaries".' },
        { options: ['The picture is not, however, entirely positive.', 'This success was equally clear in every company that took part.', 'Most workers, in fact, said they preferred a longer week.', 'Employers were therefore delighted with the results.'], answer: 0, why: '"For example" must introduce an instance of a problem, so the gap must announce that not everything was positive. B, C and D all state a general positive or opposite claim, so "exhausted rather than refreshed" could not be an example of them (and C contradicts the trials\' favourable tone).' },
        { options: ['Office work, in particular, cannot easily be compressed.', 'Others have found it works best in industries that serve the public.', 'The approach is also harder to apply in some sectors than in others.', 'Fortunately, these problems have all now been solved.'], answer: 2, why: 'The hospital and shop examples illustrate sectors where a shorter week is difficult. A mentions office work, which the examples do not illustrate; B says the opposite of the examples; D contradicts "Even so, supporters argue..." which admits continuing difficulty.' },
        { options: ['Most employers have already adopted the idea.', 'The notion that long hours guarantee good work has, after all, rarely been properly tested.', 'These trials were all conducted in small firms.', 'Workers, however, remain doubtful about longer weekends.'], answer: 1, why: '"That assumption" needs a belief or notion in the gap: that long hours guarantee good work. A contradicts "worth testing more widely" and gives no assumption; C and D give no assumption for "that assumption" to refer to.' }
      ]
    }] }
  ]
});
